    "gold_confidence": "medium",
    "gold_justification": "The text reports an RCT's secondary finding accurately: 21% vs 25%, a 4 percentage point\ndifference. The senior author frames this as a \"promising signal\" — tentative language for\na secondary finding — and explicitly calls for larger trials. The null primary outcome is\nreported honestly. Study limitations are disclosed. The evidence tier (human RCT) matches\nthe conclusion tier (human outcomes with appropriate caveats). No causal overclaim is made.\n",
    "gold_alternative_reading": "An 'overclaim' reading is defensible: the null primary outcome makes the secondary finding\nvery weak, and \"promising signal\" could be seen as optimistic framing that exceeds what\na null-primary + weak-secondary result warrants. Under a strict epistemic norm, highlighting\na 4pp secondary difference from a failed trial constitutes decorative uncertainty. However,\n0/6 model runs across two validation sprints agree with the overclaim label, and the text\ndoes stay within its evidence tier (human RCT → human results).\n",
    "gold_case_type": "borderline",
    "curator_notes": "RELABELED 2026-04-08: Gold label changed from overclaim to solid.\n\nReason: 0/6 model runs across two validation sprints (Praxis + Full 27) agree with\noverclaim label. All 3 models consistently classify as solid. The text reports human\nRCT results with human-appropriate conclusions — evidence tier matches conclusion tier.\n\"Promising signal\" + \"larger trials needed\" is seen as responsible communication by\nall models. The previous overclaim label reflected a stricter epistemic norm than\nthe text warrants.\n\nThis case now serves as a borderline-solid calibration test: can the agent recognize\nthat cautious reporting of weak results from a well-designed study is solid, not overclaim?\n"
  },
